{
  "comment": "The one table of the optional `operator` field of an accounts.json entry. infra/test/operator.test.ts runs it through parseOperator, callerArnPatterns, principalArnPatterns and operatorMayDeploy (lib/operator.ts), scripts/tests/test_operator_identity.py runs it through the same rules of scripts/lib/aws_target.py and the deploy engine's caller check, and the shell generator of scripts/setup/accounts.sh (acct_generate, which writes the field from the caller identity of `aws sts get-caller-identity`) is held to the `generate` rows: each row is a caller ARN and the operator the generator writes for it, or the reason it refuses. So the three parsers cannot drift apart. `{account}` stands for the entry's account id and `{other}` for another account's. The field names the one identity that runs setup and later operates the stage: an IAM role by its name as it appears in the assumed-role ARN (an IAM Identity Center role is named AWSReservedSSO_<permission set>_<16 hex>), or an IAM user by its user name. No path, no wildcard, no comma (control-api's OPERATOR_PRINCIPALS is comma separated).",
